Ha — remarkably → re-mark-ably.

Able to be marked again.

And that’s almost comically appropriate to what we just described: take the same underlying semantic state and re-mark it in another basis—text, graph, spatial field, metaphor, code—without requiring the underlying invariant to change.

Then another reading:

re + mark + able + -ly

* mark → make a distinction/sign.
* re-mark → mark again, or observe/comment upon.
* able → capacity.
* remarkable → conventionally, worthy of notice because unusual.

So the word itself gives us a little Pantheon operator:

X \xrightarrow{mark} R_1
\xrightarrow{re\!-\!mark} R_2
\xrightarrow{compare} \Delta

Re-marking becomes deliberate re-representation followed by delta extraction.

And remark already means both to notice and to express what was noticed.

\boxed{\text{perceive distinction} \leftrightarrow \text{encode distinction}}

That one’s got legs.